0117-372f-5362-ae3b-b45651947dbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176637 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176638 #edb/ref 1061 192126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176638 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54ebaf71-e1f2-548d-a8b8-baebf7d035a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176638 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177095 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the slope of a line that is perpendicular to a given line $y=mx+b,$ we can just take the **negative reciprocal** of the given slope $m.$ So, the slope of the perpendicular line would be $-\\dfrac{1}{m}.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $y = 3x + 1,$ which has a slope of $m=3.$ The negative reciprocal of $3$ is $-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$ So, any line with a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{3}$ will be perpendicular to the given line, because\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right) = -1.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, some lines with a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{3}$ are plotted below. We can see that they are all perpendicular to the given line $y=3x+1.$\n\n![](images/e2/e2772aa0cbe3e736e1129b52c59d3a7c36759a0ccf7b7a6b409cce09b2b22497.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177095 #edb/ref 1065 8898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177095 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6cd38c68-76bd-504d-9df9-754982a28e7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177095 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235858 #edb/ref 1080 "3x+y=-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235858 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235858 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08fb0cd4-c595-52e2-b7b8-f5921f1664ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236143 #edb/ref 1080 "3x-2y=-10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236143 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236143 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "339a3f8d-ec60-53c0-b70c-2ad1dcb8beef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237036 #edb/ref 1080 "x+2y=2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237036 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237036 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bf918fbb-8ce0-57e4-ba33-761c02c1700f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237119 #edb/ref 1080 "x-2y=-6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237119 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237119 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cd49d0d7-034b-5674-9bb1-76d06ca38e03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237390 #edb/ref 1080 "3x+2y=-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237390 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237390 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f90a028f-fc8e-54be-b155-96319e83f2b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237683 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186235858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237683 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237683 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237683 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237683 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237683 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237683 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237683 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186237390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237683 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "be8d6f01-6ff4-56d4-9645-4daf3987bc75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238009 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238009 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238009 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238009 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-2,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(4,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238009 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c2cb582-f805-50f4-ae14-8678782b90fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238009 #edb/ref 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-2,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+y & =-1 \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}x+y) & =2\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 3x+2y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481551 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481547 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481551 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481551 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-4,1)$ and is perpendicular to $5x+y=2?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481551 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52c85608-74fb-537e-b49a-ca47a66774d5" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481551 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the equation $5 x + y=2$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 x + y=2 \\\\ y &= 2 -5 x \\\\ y &= 2 -5 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. The given line has a slope of $-5,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=\\frac{1}{5}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(-4,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(1) &= \\frac{1}{5}(x-(-4)) \\\\ y-(1) &= \\frac{x}{5} + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ y &= \\frac{x}{5} + \\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481556 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481548 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481556 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481556 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2, 6)$ and $(-3, 1)$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481556 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80cf21cd-1688-5baa-bf78-1e05cbe212c4" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481556 #edb/ref 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(2,6)$ and $(-3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m_1 &= \\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \\\\ &= \\frac{1-(6)}{-3-(2)} \\\\ &= \\frac{-5}{-5} \\\\ &= 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. The given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-1$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(-3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(4) &= -1(x-(-3)) \\\\ y-(4) &= -x -3 \\\\ y &= 1 -x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 1 -x \\\\ x + y &= 1 \\\\ 1(x + y) &= 1(1) \\\\ x + y &= 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481557 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481560 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481560 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(5,6)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7aa6290-5c44-5145-bf1e-dc13974023ae" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481560 #edb/ref 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m_1 &= \\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \\\\ &= \\frac{-1-(5)}{-3-(1)} \\\\ &= \\frac{-6}{-4} \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(5,6).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(6) &= -\\frac{2}{3}(x-(5)) \\\\ y-(6) &= \\frac{10}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3} \\\\ y &= \\frac{28}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= \\frac{28}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2 x}{3} + y &= \\frac{28}{3} \\\\ 3(\\frac{2 x}{3} + y) &= 3(\\frac{28}{3}) \\\\ 2 x + 3 y &= 28\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481563 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186175924.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481563 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "823250e1-e668-5094-b72c-498bf632e690" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490396 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490395 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490396 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490396 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,3)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490396 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6fe3232-91eb-5c5b-90f0-5931f279e031" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490396 #edb/ref 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{1+1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (1)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-1.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-1(x-0) \\\\ y-1 & =-x \\\\ y & =1 -x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490397 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186175923.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490397 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "d4f8e14e-e664-5d3c-b8e6-ce7ab96b3db1" #edb/ref 17592186045443]}
